One Time Setup (Call Once at Init)
create_instance()
select_physical_device()
create_logical_device()
create_surface()
create_swapchain()
create_image_views()
setup_dynamic_rendering()
create_graphics_pipeline()
create_command_pool()
allocate_command_buffers()
create_sync_objects()        ← semaphores and fences, easy to forget

Every Frame (Call in Game Loop)
draw_frame()
    ├── wait_for_fence()              ← wait for previous frame to finish
    ├── acquire_swapchain_image()     ← get next image index
    ├── reset_command_buffer()        ← clear last frame's commands
    ├── record_command_buffer()       ← record new draw commands
    ├── submit_command_buffer()       ← submit to GPU queue
    └── present_image()              ← return image to swapchain for display

Needs to Be Re-run on Window Resize
destroy_swapchain()
create_swapchain()
create_image_views()
This is called swapchain recreation and catches people off guard. Vulkan will return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR from vkAcquireNextImageKHR or vkQueuePresentKHR when the window is resized, signaling you need to recreate:
cVkResult result = vkAcquireNextImageKHR(...);
if (result == VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR) {
    recreate_swapchain();
    return;
}

Full Picture
c// main.c

int main(void) {
    // one time init
    create_instance();
    select_physical_device();
    create_logical_device();
    create_surface();
    create_swapchain();
    create_image_views();
    setup_dynamic_rendering();
    create_graphics_pipeline();
    create_command_pool();
    allocate_command_buffers();
    create_sync_objects();

    // game loop
    while (running) {
        poll_input();
        update(delta_time);
        draw_frame();           // contains all per frame steps
    }

    // wait for GPU to finish before cleanup
    vkDeviceWaitIdle(device);

    // cleanup in reverse order of creation
    cleanup();

    return 0;
}
The vkDeviceWaitIdle before cleanup is important — you can't destroy Vulkan objects while the GPU is still using them.
